  {
   "@id": "urn:soundlore:facet:RHY",
   "@type": "skos:Collection",
   "prefLabel": "Rhythm, meter and tempo",
   "notation": "RHY",
   "definition": "This facet covers the organization, perceived movement, and speed of musical time, including beat grouping, rhythmic relationships, and changes in pace.",
   "scopeNote": "Rhythm concerns when musical events happen and how their durations and accents relate. Meter provides a recurring framework for grouping beats, while tempo describes their rate. These dimensions interact, but they are distinct: the same rhythm can be performed at different speeds, and a steady tempo can support several patterns of accent. Listeners may perceive a pulse even when no instrument explicitly marks every beat.\n\nThe facet has four main branches. Meter includes regular beat groupings, changing meters, and overlapping metrical frameworks. Rhythmic feel describes how musicians shape the movement of a passage, including straight, swung, half-time, and laid-back approaches. Rhythmic devices covers relationships such as syncopation, polyrhythm, and rhythmic displacement. Tempo includes broad speed descriptions and ways of changing or stretching the pace.\n\nThese features help distinguish musical traditions without defining them alone. Waltzes commonly organize movement around three beats, much rock emphasizes a regular pulse with prominent backbeats, and some West African ensemble traditions combine contrasting rhythmic layers. This facet describes the temporal principles behind such differences. Specific drum grooves and percussion executions belong in Drums and percussion patterns; the organization of sections belongs in Form and structure. A genre can contain several meters, feels, or tempos.",
   "member": [
