  {
   "@id": "urn:soundlore:facet:DRM",
   "@type": "skos:Collection",
   "prefLabel": "Drums and percussion patterns",
   "notation": "DRM",
   "definition": "This facet covers recurring drum and percussion grooves, along with playing techniques that shape their accents, textures, and rhythmic impact.",
   "scopeNote": "Drums and percussion give rhythm a particular physical and sonic form. A pattern distributes strikes, rests, and accents among instruments or playing surfaces. Its identity depends on more than the placement of beats: differences in touch, sound, timing, and interaction can make similar patterns feel substantially different. Percussion can establish a steady foundation, answer a melody, mark transitions, or become the main musical focus.\n\nThe facet is organized into Drum patterns and Drum techniques. Patterns include recognizable grooves such as four-on-the-floor, dembow, one-drop, and breakbeat-based approaches. Techniques describe how those patterns are performed or embellished, including ghost notes, rolls, flams, fills, brush playing, and linear drumming. A technique can appear within many patterns, while a pattern can be realized with acoustic instruments, electronic drums, or samples.\n\nPercussion is often a strong clue to genre. Reggae one-drop patterns organize emphasis differently from the continuous kick pulse common in much house music. Samba ensembles distribute rhythmic roles across several percussion instruments, creating a collective groove. These examples also show why an isolated pattern cannot identify every recording reliably. General concepts such as meter, syncopation, and tempo belong in Rhythm, meter and tempo. Instrument identities belong in Instrumentation, while processing and editing percussion sounds belong in Production and sound.",
   "member": [
