  {
   "@id": "urn:soundlore:facet:HAR",
   "@type": "skos:Collection",
   "prefLabel": "Harmony and tonality",
   "notation": "HAR",
   "definition": "This facet covers simultaneous pitch relationships, tonal organization, chord movement, musical closure, and changes between tonal centers.",
   "scopeNote": "Harmony concerns how pitches sound together and how those combinations relate over time. Tonality describes an organization in which a particular pitch or chord acts as a center of reference. Music can establish that center strongly, move between centers, combine several, or avoid a clear tonal center. Harmonic tension and resolution depend on musical context and learned expectations; they are not universal measures of emotional meaning.\n\nThe facet has six main branches. Tonality describes broad systems of pitch organization. Chords identifies simultaneous pitch combinations. Harmonic devices covers resources such as pedal points, borrowed chords, and reharmonization. Chord progressions describes recurring sequences, while Cadences concerns gestures of arrival or closure. Modulation covers movement between tonal centers. These branches connect individual sonorities with the larger direction of a piece.\n\nHarmony helps distinguish styles through customary resources and their use. Western classical repertories often organize phrases around tonal movement and cadences. Jazz frequently develops extended chords and reharmonization, while blues makes distinctive use of recurring harmonic patterns. These are tendencies rather than requirements for every piece. Pitch collections considered as scales or modes belong in Scales and modes. The movement of a melodic line belongs in Melody and motif, and the interaction of simultaneous parts belongs in Texture and counterpoint.",
