  {
   "@id": "urn:soundlore:facet:SCL",
   "@type": "skos:Collection",
   "prefLabel": "Scales and modes",
   "notation": "SCL",
   "definition": "This facet covers pitch collections and modal frameworks used to organize melodies, harmonies, and characteristic musical relationships.",
   "scopeNote": "Scales provide ways of arranging pitches, often presented in ascending or descending order. Modes describe pitch organization through relationships to a center and characteristic degrees or intervals. A collection of notes does not determine how music will sound by itself: emphasis, melodic movement, tuning, ornamentation, and harmonic context all affect its identity. Two pieces using similar pitch material can therefore have very different characters.\n\nThe facet is organized into Scales, Modes, and Modal and tuning systems. The first two branches include major and minor resources, pentatonic collections, symmetrical scales, named modes, and scales such as the Phrygian dominant that are shared by several traditions. The third holds frameworks that go beyond a list of pitches. Maqam and raga, for example, also involve hierarchies of degrees, characteristic phrases, ornamentation, and intonation, while the gamelan tunings pelog and slendro are properties of a particular ensemble's instruments.\n\nPitch frameworks help describe differences among traditions. Major and minor resources are widespread in Western tonal music; pentatonic collections occur in many musical cultures; Indian classical performance develops melodies within raga frameworks. None of these resources belongs exclusively to one genre. This facet describes the pitch material and its organizing framework. Chord function and tonal movement belong in Harmony and tonality, while the actual shaping, ornamentation, and development of melodic lines belong in Melody and motif.",
   "member": [
