  {
   "@id": "urn:soundlore:facet:MEL",
   "@type": "skos:Collection",
   "prefLabel": "Melody and motif",
   "notation": "MEL",
   "definition": "This facet covers recognizable musical lines, recurring melodic ideas, ways of transforming or decorating them, and practices of improvisation.",
   "scopeNote": "A melody is a sequence of pitches and durations heard as a connected line. It may be sung, played by an instrument, or distributed among several performers. A motif is a shorter idea that can recur or develop within a piece. Melodic identity depends on rhythm and phrasing as well as pitch, so changing an idea’s timing can alter its character even when its notes remain similar.\n\nThe facet has three main branches. Melodic elements includes hooks, riffs, motifs, ostinatos, countermelodies, and other recognizable materials. Melodic devices describes ways of shaping a line, including sequences, chromatic movement, ornaments, and different relationships between successive notes. Improvisation concerns the creation or development of musical material during performance, including practices that respond to an established harmonic or modal framework.\n\nMelodic practices help distinguish traditions and styles. A rock riff can supply a recurring instrumental identity, a jazz solo can develop ideas through improvisation, and Indian classical performance can explore characteristic melodic movements within a raga. The significance of each example lies in how material is used, rather than in the presence of melody alone. Scales and modal frameworks belong in Scales and modes. Vocal production belongs in Voice and vocal technique, while the placement of melodic ideas across a whole piece belongs in Form and structure.",
   "member": [
