  {
   "@id": "urn:soundlore:facet:FRM",
   "@type": "skos:Collection",
   "prefLabel": "Form and structure",
   "notation": "FRM",
   "definition": "This facet covers the arrangement of musical sections, patterns of repetition and contrast, and established frameworks for complete compositions.",
   "scopeNote": "Form describes how a piece unfolds over time. Listeners recognize beginnings, returns, contrasts, transitions, and endings through changes in melody, harmony, rhythm, texture, or sound. A structure may follow a familiar pattern or emerge through gradual development. Understanding form helps explain why a passage feels like preparation, arrival, interruption, or return, even when the listener cannot name the sections involved.\n\nThe facet is organized into Song sections, Song forms, and Compositional forms. Song sections identify parts such as verses, choruses, bridges, introductions, breakdowns, and outros. Song forms describe how such parts are arranged and repeated, including verse–chorus and strophic designs. Compositional forms cover broader established frameworks and types of composition, including fugues, rondos, suites, concertos, and other resources used across different repertories.\n\nFormal habits can provide clues to genre. Many popular songs alternate verses and choruses; some electronic dance arrangements organize anticipation around build-ups and drops; Western classical works may use recurring themes within a rondo or a larger sonata framework. Individual works can adapt or combine these practices. This facet concerns the placement and relationship of musical material across time. The material itself belongs in the relevant melodic, harmonic, rhythmic, or textural facet. Recording effects that create a transition belong in Production and sound, even when their structural role is described here.",
   "member": [
