  {
   "@id": "urn:soundlore:facet:TEX",
   "@type": "skos:Collection",
   "prefLabel": "Texture and counterpoint",
   "notation": "TEX",
   "definition": "This facet covers how simultaneous musical parts combine, interact, and distribute melodic or accompanying roles.",
   "scopeNote": "Texture describes the overall relationship among musical parts sounding at the same time. Music may present one line, several closely coordinated parts, independent lines, or multiple versions of a shared melody. Texture also concerns density and the distribution of attention: a few widely spaced sounds can feel different from many overlapping parts. The number of instruments alone does not determine the texture.\n\nThe facet has three main branches. Texture includes broad configurations such as monophony, homophony, polyphony, and heterophony, alongside descriptions of density. Counterpoint concerns the interaction of distinct lines, including imitation and voice leading. Accompaniment textures describes ways of supporting or responding to a principal part, such as comping, drones, repeated figures, call and response, and antiphonal exchange.\n\nTextural habits can help distinguish musical traditions. Western choral hymns may coordinate voices in chordal movement. Jazz accompaniment can leave space around an improvised lead while contributing its own rhythmic and harmonic activity. Some Indonesian gamelan repertories combine interlocking and layered parts. Such relationships may change within a single piece. This facet describes what the parts do together. Their instrument identities belong in Instrumentation, their pitch combinations in Harmony and tonality, and their individual melodic ideas in Melody and motif. Studio layering belongs here when describing the resulting musical relationship, and in Production and sound when describing the recording process.",
