  {
   "@id": "urn:soundlore:facet:VOC",
   "@type": "skos:Collection",
   "prefLabel": "Voice and vocal technique",
   "notation": "VOC",
   "definition": "This facet covers singing and spoken vocal sounds, voice categories, expressive delivery, and the organization of vocal parts.",
   "scopeNote": "The human voice can carry melody, rhythm, language, and expressive character at the same time. Vocal sound depends on how a performer coordinates production, resonance, articulation, and phrasing. Descriptions such as breathy, clean, shouted, or melismatic identify different aspects of delivery rather than fixed categories of singer. A performer may use several approaches within one song, and vocal terminology varies among musical traditions.\n\nThe facet is organized into five main branches. Voice types describes categories based on range, timbre, or performance role. Singing techniques covers approaches such as falsetto, belting, vibrato, and overtone singing. Harsh vocals groups deliberately rough or distorted vocal timbres. Spoken and rapped vocals concerns speech-centered and rhythmically organized delivery. Vocal arrangement describes the relationship among lead voices, backing parts, harmonies, and groups.\n\nVocal practices are often useful clues to style. Opera commonly emphasizes sustained, projected singing; many R&B performances use melismatic ornamentation; rap foregrounds the rhythmic placement and articulation of words. These practices also travel across genre boundaries. This facet describes vocal delivery and the organization of voices, rather than the literary content of the words, which belongs in Lyrics and prosody. Electronic pitch correction, vocoding, and recorded vocal distortion belong in Production and sound when the focus is the processing method. Melodic shapes shared with instrumental music belong in Melody and motif.",
   "member": [
