  {
   "@id": "urn:soundlore:facet:INS",
   "@type": "skos:Collection",
   "prefLabel": "Instrumentation",
   "notation": "INS",
   "definition": "This facet covers the instruments, sound-producing resources, and ensembles used to perform or construct music.",
   "scopeNote": "Instrumentation describes which sound-producing resources take part in music. These may include acoustic instruments, electronic devices, percussion, or established ensembles. Instruments contribute characteristic ranges, attacks, resonances, and possibilities of expression, but their identities do not prescribe a single musical role. A guitar can provide melody, chords, bass movement, or percussion-like gestures, and electronic instruments can imitate familiar sounds or produce unfamiliar ones.\n\nThe facet is organized into Plucked strings, Bowed strings, Woodwinds, Brass, Keyboards, Electronic instruments, Percussion, and Ensembles. These branches provide practical routes through instrument families and performance groupings. They are browsing categories rather than one universal scientific classification: keyboard instruments, for example, produce sound through several different mechanisms. Electronic resources also include recognizable devices and sounds associated with particular production practices.\n\nInstrument combinations can strongly suggest a tradition. A string quartet identifies a familiar Western chamber ensemble; mariachi often combines strings with trumpets; Indonesian gamelan ensembles prominently use tuned percussion. Similar instruments may nevertheless appear in very different genres, and substitutions can preserve a musical role while changing its sound. This facet identifies the resources involved. How performers play them belongs in Instrumental techniques, the relationship among their parts in Texture and counterpoint, and recording or processing choices in Production and sound. Vocal delivery is developed separately in Voice and vocal technique.",
