  {
   "@id": "urn:soundlore:facet:TEC",
   "@type": "skos:Collection",
   "prefLabel": "Instrumental techniques",
   "notation": "TEC",
   "definition": "This facet covers ways of playing and manipulating instruments that shape articulation, pitch, rhythm, timbre, and musical expression.",
   "scopeNote": "Instrumental technique describes the actions through which a performer produces and shapes sound. Different gestures can change a note’s attack, duration, pitch, resonance, or rhythmic emphasis. Techniques may be associated with a particular instrument, but related musical effects can arise through different physical methods. A connected phrase, for example, requires different actions on a guitar, a bowed string instrument, and a wind instrument.\n\nThe facet is organized into Guitar techniques, Bass techniques, Keyboard techniques, Bowed-string techniques, Wind techniques, and DJ techniques. These branches include picking and muting approaches, bass articulation, keyboard accompaniment patterns, bowing and plucking gestures, breath-related effects, and the manipulation of recorded material through turntables. Some entries describe recurring performance approaches, while others identify a particular gesture or sound-producing method.\n\nTechniques help give genres their recognizable detail. Flamenco guitar makes distinctive use of strumming and plucking approaches such as rasgueado and picado. Jazz bass often supplies motion through walking lines. Hip-hop turntablism uses scratching and related manipulation as musical performance. These resources can be adopted outside their customary settings. This facet focuses on performance methods. Instrument identities belong in Instrumentation, broadly applicable articulations belong in Dynamics and articulation, and audio processing such as compression, filtering, or reverberation belongs in Production and sound. A processed sound may combine contributions from both playing technique and studio treatment.",
