  {
   "@id": "urn:soundlore:facet:DYN",
   "@type": "skos:Collection",
   "prefLabel": "Dynamics and articulation",
   "notation": "DYN",
   "definition": "This facet covers expressive changes in musical intensity and the ways notes are attacked, sustained, connected, or separated.",
   "scopeNote": "Dynamics concerns the relative strength or intensity of musical events. Articulation concerns how notes begin, continue, and end, including their connection or separation. Together, they shape phrasing and emphasis: the same sequence of pitches can sound restrained, forceful, flowing, or sharply marked depending on its execution. Their effects depend on musical context, instrument, and the contrasts established within a passage.\n\nThe facet has two main branches. Dynamics includes quiet and loud performance, gradual increases and decreases, sudden emphasis, and contrasts between passages. Articulation includes approaches such as staccato, legato, marcato, and tenuto. These terms describe relationships within the music rather than fixed acoustic measurements. A quiet passage can remain intense, while a strong attack need not produce a long or sustained sound.\n\nExpressive handling helps distinguish performances and styles. Western classical repertories often use carefully shaped dynamic contrasts and articulation. Jazz phrasing can depend on subtle accents and differences in note length. Rock arrangements may create dramatic contrast between restrained verses and forceful choruses. No single pattern defines all examples of a genre. This facet describes musical expression through intensity and note shaping. Compression and limiting belong in Production and sound as signal-processing methods. Instrument-specific ways of achieving an articulation belong in Instrumental techniques, and emotional interpretations of the result belong in Mood and character.",
   "member": [
    "sl:SL000719",
    "sl:SL000720",
    "sl:SL000721",
    "sl:SL000722",
    "sl:SL000723",
    "sl:SL000724",
    "sl:SL000725",
    "sl:SL000726",
    "sl:SL000727",
    "sl:SL000728",
    "sl:SL000729",
    "sl:SL000730",
    "sl:SL000731"
   ]
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:soundlore:facet:LYR",
   "@type": "skos:Collection",
   "prefLabel": "Lyrics and prosody",
   "notation": "LYR",
   "definition": "This facet covers the verbal construction of songs, including rhyme, sound patterns, narrative devices, and the relationship between words and musical delivery.",
   "scopeNote": "Lyrics contribute language, imagery, narrative, and verbal sound to music. Prosody concerns how features such as syllables, stress, rhythm, and phrasing interact with musical delivery. Words can align closely with musical accents or create expressive tension against them. Their meaning also changes through repetition, placement, and performance, so a lyric’s musical effect cannot be understood from its written text alone.\n\nThe facet is organized around Lyrical devices. This branch includes rhyme schemes, internal and multisyllabic rhyme, assonance, repetition, storytelling, wordplay, vocables, and other ways of shaping verbal material. It also includes broad approaches to lyrical content, such as protest, confession, and abstraction. These entries describe resources that can coexist: a narrative song can use repeated phrases, intricate rhyme, and ambiguous imagery at the same time.\n\nLyrical practices help distinguish traditions and individual styles. Rap often gives particular prominence to rhyme placement and verbal rhythm. Folk ballads frequently organize words around a developing story. Some flamenco singing stretches or repeats words for expressive emphasis. These practices are shared and adapted across genres. This facet concerns the words and their organization in music. Vocal timbre and delivery belong in Voice and vocal technique, while melodic ornamentation belongs in Melody and motif. The facet also records the absence of words: Instrumental marks pieces without sung or spoken lyrics.",
   "member": [
    "sl:SL000732",
    "sl:SL000733",
    "sl:SL000734",
